answer:
Using `+` in a loop creates a new "String" object on every iteration because "String" is immutable, which is O(n^2) overall.

```java
// Inefficient: creates a new String each iteration
String result = "";
for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
    result += i;
}
```

Prefer "StringBuilder", which mutates an internal buffer instead of allocating a new object each time:

```java
// Efficient: mutates a single buffer
StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
    sb.append(i);
}
String result = sb.toString();
```
